mb|P|mb|P|mb|3S|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I would open West hand 3@S in first 3 seats, but it is particularly effective in 3rd seat at favorable vul (you are not vul, they are vul). |
PG| ***** 17 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|And, in fact, at favorable vulnerability (you are not vulnerable, they are vulnerable), it would be reasonable to consider opening 4@S to make it harder for the opponents to find their possible heart fit. You should be particularly aggressive in preempting when you are in 3rd seat at favorable vulnerability.|
PG| ***** 18 ***** |


mb|D|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|If I were not a passed hand, double by North is probably best since there is even some chance I would pass for penalties. The main issue is that it doesn't get across the 5 card heart suit.|
PG| ***** 19 ***** |


mb|P|mb|4H|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|4S|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I think West intentionally meant to bid 4@S. I will discuss the 3@S and 4@S bid by West after. There are many issues to talk about.|
PG| ***** 20 ***** |


