UP|52|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|First, let's discuss what East might do over the double. A good rule to follow is ^*Byou should bid 4@S over 4@H if you have a 9+ card fit^*n. So East, with no defense and a 9 card fit, and knowing we likely have a heart fit (since West rates to have at most 2 hearts after preempting) should definitely consider bidding 4@S.|
PG| ***** 32 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|As to West now bidding 4@S over 4@H. In general, once you preempt, you should not bid again, particularly if partner passes. But in this case, with a singleton heart, very nice spades, and at favorable vul, I don't mind the 4@S bid although it is technically not what West is supposed to do. Having said that, I think, even if West passed, East should bid 4@S over 4@H.|
PG| ***** 33 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Now let us go back to whether West should open 3@S or 1@S to begin with. The hand does meet the rule of |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=ruleof20,Rule Of 20^*N|CP||FP||at|. So technically 1@S is a possible opening bid.|
PG| ***** 34 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Let us count West's ^*BAdjusted Bergen Rule of 20 Points^*n.  ^*BAdjusted Bergen Rule of 20 Points^*n are what you use for |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=ruleof20,Rule Of 20^*N|CP||FP||at| calculations.|
PG| ***** 35 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|They are basically all of Bergen's |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=bergenstartingpoints,Bergen Starting Points^*N|CP||FP||at| points except length (because length is taken care of by "the length of 2 longest suits" that is part of the Rule.)|
PG| ***** 36 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|West has 10 HCP, -1 for Jx of diamonds, and + 1 for quality 3 honor spade suit for 10 ^*BAdjusted Bergen Points^*n. West's can add ^*BAdjusted Bergen Points^*n and 10 cards in the black suits to meet the |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=ruleof20,Rule Of 20^*N|CP||FP||at|.|
PG| ***** 37 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Another good feature of the hand is all the points (except for the @DJ) are in West's 2 longest suits. All in all, I would still open 3@S in all seats and I certainly think in 3rd seat 3@S is better than 1@S because you want to preempt 4th seat who is known to be relatively strong after the 2 passes. However, it would be okay to open the hand 1@S in 1st and 2nd seat.|
PG| ***** 38 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^^B@2^*BWest: ^*nHow about in 4th seat?|
PG| ***** 39 ***** |


cp|8|AT|
^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|4th seat is interesting. You don't preempt in 4th seat. But I wouldn't want to pass with your hand. For example, you open 2@S in 4th seat, that is NOT a weak-2. It shows a minimum opening hand with a nice 6-card suit. So 2@S, not weak, is possible with the West hand in 4th seat. Similarly, West could open 3@S in 4th seat, which shows a minimum opener with 7 nice spades.|
PG| ***** 40 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|In hearts, we take 5 hearts, 3 diamonds, and 1 club and 1 club ruff for 10 tricks. Possibly, I can make 5 though it would not occur to me to bid it. 5 level decisions are very difficult. Most players bid "5 over 5" way too often. The adage "the 5 level belongs to the opponents" is a good one and is not followed often enough. That is why bidding 4@S over 4@H with a 9 card fit is usually right.|
PG| ***** 41 ***** |


